<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Get Cool in the Shade – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Get Cool in the Shade.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write instructions for playing a simple classroom game. Interactive: Write instructions for peers about how to improve their writing products.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple instructions for a game (e.g., "First, create energy-saving ideas. Then, act them out and ask peers to guess them."). Can write instructions to help peers improve their writing by explaining steps clearly (e.g., "Step 1: Add more details about conservation." "Step 2: Use adjectives like 'helpful' or 'important' to make your ideas clearer." "Step 3: Check for capital letters and punctuation."). Can give step-by-step instructions (e.g., "Use natural sunlight to dry the clothes and use energy-efficient light bulbs.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of common daily activities (e.g., a child turning off a light, a person watering plants, clothes drying in the sun). Students work in pairs to write down two words or short phrases that describe each picture, focusing on actions related to "save" or "waste." (e.g., "Turn off," "Save water").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple imperative instructions on the board using one of the pictures. For example, for the light switch picture, the teacher writes: "Turn off the light. Don't waste electricity." The teacher highlights the imperative verbs (e.g., "Turn off," "Don't waste") and stresses the /t/ sound in "turn off" and introduces simple prefixes like -re in "recycle."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'Turn off the light' tell us to do?" (Expected answer: "Close the light," "Make it dark.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are these sentences telling us about the past, present, or telling us to do something?" (Expected answer: "Telling us to do something.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short set of written instructions for a simple conservation-themed classroom game called "Energy Savers Guessing Game." The instructions are written clearly on a poster or projected. Example: "First, create energy-saving ideas. Then, act them out and ask peers to guess them. It is *easier* to guess if you use actions. Remember to *turn off* the lights to get cool in the *shade*." (Genre: Instructions, Register: Simple and direct, Format: Numbered or bulleted list).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will learn to understand and identify simple, step-by-step written instructions related to conservation and game playing, using imperative verbs and target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the instructions individually. Then, in pairs, they write down the imperative verbs they find (e.g., "create," "act out," "ask," "turn off").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives students cut-out strips of the instructions, and students write the correct number (1, 2, 3...) next to each strip to put them in the correct order.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing imperative sentences to give instructions, using target vocabulary (shade, light, water, energy, recycle, turn off, save, cool, easy, bright, safe) and comparatives/superlatives (easier, safer, most). Pronunciation of /sh/ in "shade" and /t/ in "turn off" will be reinforced orally when reading their written work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-Blanks):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students write in the missing imperative verbs or target vocabulary words to complete conservation instructions. Example: "1. ____ off the lights. 2. ____ water when brushing your teeth. 3. Let's ____ waste. 4. Use ____ power, it's ____." (Answers: Turn, Save, recycle, solar, cooler/safer).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Transformation):</b> Students are given simple statements and asked to rewrite them as imperative instructions, incorporating comparative or superlative adjectives where possible. Example: "We should use less electricity at night." → "Turn off lights at night. It's *easier* to save energy." Or "It's good to keep the room cool." → "Let's open the window to get cool in the *shade*. It's *safer* than using too much AC."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Collaborative Instructions):</b> In pairs, students choose one simple daily conservation action (e.g., recycling, saving water, using natural light) and collaboratively write 3-4 steps of instructions on how to do it. They must use at least two imperative verbs and two target vocabulary words. Example: "How to save water: 1. Turn off the *faucet* when you don't need *water*. 2. *Recycle* water for plants. 3. It's *important* to conserve *water*."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will individually write a set of instructions for a new simple classroom game focused on conservation, similar to the "Energy Savers Guessing Game" but with their own ideas. They will then exchange their written game instructions with a peer and provide written instructions for improvement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1 (Productive Writing):</b> Students think of a simple classroom game related to "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!" (e.g., "Shade Seekers," "Recycle Relay"). They write down 3-5 step-by-step instructions for their game. They must use at least three imperative verbs (e.g., "find," "collect," "switch"), three target vocabulary words (e.g., "shade," "energy," "light," "water"), and one comparative (e.g., "easier," "safer") or superlative (e.g., "most important") adjective. They are encouraged to use phrases like "First," "Next," "Then."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2 (Interactive Writing - Peer Feedback):</b> Students exchange their written game instructions with a partner. Each student then writes specific feedback/instructions to their peer on how to improve their game instructions. They should use phrases like: "Step 1: Add more details about conservation." "Step 2: Use adjectives like 'helpful' or 'important' to make your ideas clearer." "Step 3: Check for capital letters and punctuation."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the writing process and a simple peer feedback checklist. Students will use the peer feedback they received to revise their game instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will submit their revised game instructions along with the written peer feedback they received. The teacher will also collect the written instructions for improving their peer's writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and logical flow of the written game instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of at least three imperative verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integration of at least three target vocabulary words (e.g., "shade," "energy," "water," "recycle," "bulbs").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least one comparative or superlative (e.g., "easier," "most important").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to provide clear, step-by-step written instructions for peer improvement (e.g., "Step 1: Add...", "Step 2: Check...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Basic grammar and punctuation (capital letters, periods).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write short answers to the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today in my writing is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about writing instructions is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing instructions was the *easiest* for me, and what was the *most important* to remember?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use what I learned today to write instructions for other things, like helping at home?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of guiding students to write clear instructions and provide constructive peer feedback? Did students successfully use imperative verbs and comparative/superlative adjectives in their writing? What adjustments are needed for scaffolding sentence structure or vocabulary usage in future writing tasks, especially for students who struggled with sequencing or grammar? The peer feedback component was effective in promoting interactive writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on producing and refining written instructions. The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build upon these writing skills by having students consolidate their understanding of conservation through a larger project, possibly involving summarizing information or creating a campaign poster with slogans and instructions, drawing on the vocabulary and grammar practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>